    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and it passes. Compilation across the whole project succeeded (the build compiles all sources, including the new component and the modified service).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: on successful owner create, enqueue a welcome notification by emitting a line via the logger named `NOTIFY` containing the owner id and the memberId.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **New `WelcomeNotifier` component** (`notification/WelcomeNotifier.java`) \u2014 a small, single-responsibility Spring `@Component` that mirrors the existing `OwnerAuditLogger` pattern. It owns a `NOTIFY` SLF4J logger and exposes `welcome(Owner)`, emitting `id=<ownerId> memberId=<memberId>`. Notification concerns stay out of the business logic, just as audit concerns do.\n\n2. **`ClinicServiceImpl`** \u2014 injected the `WelcomeNotifier` via the constructor and invoked `welcomeNotifier.welcome(owner)` right after `ownerAuditLogger.ownerCreated(owner)` in `createOwner`, so it fires only on a successful, persisted create (inside the existing `@Transactional` happy path, after all validation/limit checks).\n\nI reused the existing owner id and `memberId` already computed and persisted in the create flow rather than duplicating any logic, and kept each concern in its own unit.",
